  "summary": "Seven preprints link MRI-derived structure and resting signal variability to development, aging, sensory and reading differences, and adversity-related psychosis risk.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is MRI phenotyping that relates individual differences in brain structure or intrinsic signal dynamics to age, cognition and clinical status. Several preprints examine moment-to-moment properties rather than mean activation: BOLD variability and altered brain-behavior covariance in adults with dyslexia, lower resting entropy in right superior temporal gyrus in hearing loss, and age-related changes in resting signal measures tied to inhibitory control, especially contextual control, in youths aged 8–18. Structural studies span the lifespan: nonlinear, region-specific cerebellar atrophy (strongest posteriorly, with midlife vulnerability) in 1,371 adults, and choroid plexus volume that rises with age and is larger in males across 700 manual segmentations. In adolescents, symmetric ACC sulcal patterns relate to lower proactive and greater reactive control. Bullying victimisation alters medial occipital white matter maturation and moderates FA–psychotic-like experience links. Samples outside the aging and choroid plexus datasets are modest (52–101 participants).",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
